Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 374, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Known for making everything from cockpit controls to N95 masks, Honeywell dipped two spots, to No. 94, on this year\u2019s list. Revenues fell by 11.1% year over year, to $32.6 billion, as the pandemic grounded flights. Travel bans pummeled the company\u2019s biggest business, aerospace, which includes selling commercial airplane parts to customers like Boeing and Airbus. Honeywell partially offset that turbulence by beefing up its warehouse automation unit, which supplies Amazon and others, and by spinning up new manufacturing lines for much in demand personal protective equipment. By the end of 2020, Honeywell had an enviable $15.2 billion in cash and equivalents on the balance sheet, which it could use for acquisitions. &quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">Known for making everything from cockpit controls to N95 masks, Honeywell dipped 27 spots, to No. 374, on this year\u2019s Global 500 list. Revenues fell by 11.1% year over year, to $32.6 billion, as the pandemic grounded flights. Travel bans pummeled the company\u2019s biggest business, aerospace, which includes selling commercial airplane parts to customers like Boeing and Airbus. Honeywell partially offset that turbulence by beefing up its warehouse automation unit, which supplies Amazon and others, and by spinning up new manufacturing lines for much in demand personal protective equipment. By the end of 2020, Honeywell had an enviable $15.2 billion in cash and equivalents on the balance sheet, which it could use for acquisitions.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 94, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Known for making everything from cockpit controls to N95 masks, Honeywell dipped two spots, to No. 94, on this year\u2019s list. Revenues fell by 11.1% year over year, to $32.6 billion, as the pandemic grounded flights. Travel bans pummeled the company\u2019s biggest business, aerospace, which includes selling commercial airplane parts to customers like Boeing and Airbus. Honeywell partially offset that turbulence by beefing up its warehouse automation unit, which supplies Amazon and others, and by spinning up new manufacturing lines for much in demand personal protective equipment. By the end of 2020, Honeywell had an enviable $15.2 billion in cash and equivalents on the balance sheet, which it could use for acquisitions. &quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">Known for making everything from cockpit controls to N95 masks, Honeywell dipped two spots, to No. 94, on this year\u2019s list. Revenues fell by 11.1% year over year, to $32.6 billion, as the pandemic grounded flights. Travel bans pummeled the company\u2019s biggest business, aerospace, which includes selling commercial airplane parts to customers like Boeing and Airbus. Honeywell partially offset that turbulence by beefing up its warehouse automation unit, which supplies Amazon and others, and by spinning up new manufacturing lines for much in demand personal protective equipment. By the end of 2020, Honeywell had an enviable $15.2 billion in cash and equivalents on the balance sheet, which it could use for acquisitions. </span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 347, "itemDescription": "Known for making everything from cockpit controls to quantum computers to N95 masks, Honeywell has gotten leaner lately. The industrial giant plunged 57 places on this year\u2019s list as revenues fell by 12.2% year-over-year, to $36.7 billion. Honeywell planned the thinning: In October 2018, it jettisoned its historic home thermostat unit, now called Residio, and its automotive turbocharger business, Garret Motion. (Both spinoffs are now Fortune 1000 companies.) Thanks in part to the pruning, Honeywell has an enviable balance sheet, with about $10 billion in cash at the ready. Analysts say CEO Darius Adamczyk could well use the funds to bolster Honeywell\u2019s burgeoning warehouse automation or \u201cInternet of things\u201d software businesses with \u201cbolt-on\u201d acquisitions. But the sharp pullback in air travel, an outcome of the globally spreading coronavirus, is expected to hammer the company\u2019s biggest division, aerospace, in the fiscal quarters ahead."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 92, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-formula-bar-text-style=\"font-size:13px;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;text-decoration:none;font-family:'Arial';font-style:normal;text-decoration-skip-ink:none;\">Known for making everything from cockpit controls to quantum computers to N95 masks, Honeywell has gotten leaner lately. The industrial giant plunged 15 places, to No. 92, on this year\u2019s list as revenues fell by 12.2% year-over-year, to $36.7 billion. Honeywell planned the thinning: In October 2018, it jettisoned its historic home thermostat unit, now called Residio, and its automotive turbocharger business, Garret Motion. (Both spinoffs are now </span><em>Fortune</em><span data-sheets-formula-bar-text-style=\"font-size:13px;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;text-decoration:none;font-family:'Arial';font-style:normal;text-decoration-skip-ink:none;\"> 1000 companies.) Thanks in part to the pruning, Honeywell has an enviable balance sheet, with about $10 billion in cash at the ready. Analysts expect CEO Darius Adamczyk could use the funds to bolster Honeywell\u2019s burgeoning warehouse automation or \u201cInternet of things\u201d software businesses with \u201cbolt-on\u201d acquisitions. But halted air travel, an outcome of the globally spreading coronavirus, is expected to hammer the company\u2019s biggest division, aerospace, in the fiscal quarters ahead.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 290, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 77, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 275, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 77, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 260, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 73, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2016, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 256, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 75, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2015, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2015, "rank": 275, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortun